  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nQuiet weather is expected through the next seven days as surface \nhigh pressure remains dominant across the region. Guidance is in \ngood agreement through the end of the week, showing upper ridging \namplifying as it builds eastward across the region. Large scale \nsubsidence will continue to keep the forecast dry. A weak cold front \nmay push in from the northeast Thursday Night into Friday. The \nboundary appears very weak with temperatures only expected to drop a \nfew degrees at most. \n\nTemperatures will slightly warm over the weekend due to return flow \nas surface high pressure shifts east of central Indiana. Highs in \nthe upper 60s to low 70s Friday should warm into the low-mid 70s by \nSunday. Long range guidance begins to diverge regarding the upper\nlevel pattern towards the end of the weekend. A southern stream \nwave will likely get pulled northward and absorbed into the \nnorthern stream trough. Models differ on the overall evolution of \nthese two features which leads to greater uncertainty in the extended\nforecast.\n\nA cold front associated with the northern stream trough should\neventually move through central Indiana around the early to \nmiddle of next week, but confidence on specific details such as \ntiming remain limited. There also appears to be a non-zero chance \nfor light precipitation from the approaching boundary. Some \nguidance depicts a narrow band of sufficient moisture surging \nnorth ahead of the boundary and weak forcing supporting the \npotential. These solutions remain the outlier though with most \nguidance suggesting the limited moisture return will not be enough\nto overcome lingering dry air. The forecast remains completely dry\nfor the time being until guidance becomes better aligned. Once \nthe front eventually moves through, a cooler trend towards \nseasonal temperatures can be expected.",
